  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nShowers continue across southeast Montana and portions of \nnorthern Wyoming as an upper low progresses through Wyoming today.\nRainfall rates are not expected to be as high as the past few \ndays, though heavier showers and a few thunderstorms this \nafternoon could produce locally heavy rainfall in southeast \nMontana and northern Wyoming. Additional rainfall up to a few \ntenths of an inch are forecast into Monday morning. The greatest \naccumulations are likely to occur along and south of US-212. \nLighter showers over south- central Montana could bring a few \nhundredths of an inch of additional rainfall through tonight.\n\nFlood Watches remain in effect until 6 PM MDT due to the \naforementioned risk of locally heavy rainfall this afternoon. The \ngreatest risk exists mainly east of Billings.\n\nPatchy to areas of fog are shown developing by several models\ntonight, the forecast shows this as well. There is uncertainty on\nfog developing versus low stratus prevailing, mainly for \nlocations west of Billings. Fog could become dense at times into \nMonday morning, especially for areas that received a lot of \nrainfall over the past few days. This will need to be monitored\novernight as products may be required if fog becomes enough of an\nissue.\n\nLooking ahead, the pattern will get warmer with highs in the 70s \nMonday through Wednesday. Conditions also look to become drier, \nthough a shortwave moving through Wednesday morning may bring \nelevated showers and maybe a few thunderstorms to southeast \nMontana. Otherwise, an additional upper low is shown by ensembles \ndigging into the Pacific Northwest to end the week. This could \nbring more precipitation to the area as well as cooler \ntemperatures. The latest trends have this system tracking through \nnorthern Montana which could leave our area in flux for \nprecipitation amounts. Continue to monitor the forecast as the \nweek progresses. Matos",
